Q1. Explain the problem in your own words.
We are given an array of characters. We need to compress consecutive repeating characters by replacing them with the character followed by its count if the count is greater than one.
The compression must be done in-place, and we return the new length of the compressed array.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q2. What does "in-place" mean?
"In-place" means we are not allowed to create another array to store the result
Instead,
Original Array
↓
Modify the same array
↓
Return new length
Example
Before
[a,a,a,b,b,c]
After
[a,3,b,2,c]
Notice
The original array itself is modified.
------------------------------------
Q3. Why can't we use only one pointer?
Because one pointer cannot safely perform both reading and writing.
Suppose
a a a a b
If we start writing while reading,
we may overwrite characters that haven't been processed yet.
Using two pointers separates responsibilities.
Read Pointer
↓
Reads original data
Write Pointer
↓
Writes compressed data
This prevents data loss.
-----------------------

Q4. Why do we need two pointers?
Each pointer has a different job.
Read Pointer
Reads the original array.
Counts consecutive characters.
Never writes.
Write Pointer
Writes the compressed result.
Never decides how many characters are repeated.
Keeping these responsibilities separate makes the algorithm correct and easier to understand.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

