Q14. Why use
write++
instead of
++write
In this case, both work because the increment is not part of a larger expression whose return value is used.
With
chars[write++] = currentChar;
the process is:
Use the current value of write as the index.
Store the character.
Increase write by 1.
Example:
write = 2
↓
chars[2] = 'a'
↓
write becomes 3
Using chars[++write] would increment first and then write at the next index, skipping the current position.
